---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vac",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Air Purifiers for Allergies",
    "url": "/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Vinyl Plank Flooring",
    "url": "/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Robot Vacuums in 2026: Tested on Real, Cluttered Floors";
const description =
  "Six robot vacuums compared on obstacle avoidance, brush design, mop pressure and dock functions \u2014 plus the consumable costs and privacy settings nobody mentions.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "roborock-saros-10r",
    "name": "Roborock Saros 10R",
    "category": "LiDAR + Dual Vision, Full Omni Dock",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 3200,
    "pros": [
      "Obstacle avoidance is the best available \u2014 it navigated cables, socks and a deliberately placed phone charger without a single pickup",
      "Under 3 inches tall, so it fits beneath furniture that strands most flagship robots",
      "Dock washes the mop pads in hot water and dries them, which is what actually stops the sour smell",
      "Dual anti-tangle brush design handled long hair far better than any single-roller robot in this test",
      "Mapping is fast, accurate, and the room-level controls are the most usable in the category"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Expensive, and the dock is large enough that finding a home for it takes real thought",
      "Consumables \u2014 pads, filters, brushes, dock bags \u2014 add up to a meaningful annual cost"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most capable robot vacuum you can buy, and the one least likely to need rescuing. If the budget stretches, it is the right answer.",
    "description": "The specification that determines whether a robot vacuum is a help or a chore is not suction; it is how rarely you have to go and find it. On that measure the Saros 10R is clearly the best machine we have used. Across a month in a house with cables, rugs, pet bowls, and a small child's floor debris, it required rescuing once, and that was a sock it had genuinely tried to avoid. The dual vision plus LiDAR arrangement builds a reliable map quickly and then avoids objects at close range rather than nudging them around the room. Its low profile is more useful than it sounds. At under three inches tall it slides under the sofa and bed frames where most flagship robots wedge themselves, which is exactly where dust accumulates and where you least want to be crawling. The dual anti-tangle brush design was the standout in a house with long hair; where single-roller robots needed cutting free every week or two, this went the full test period without intervention. The dock does the whole job: empties the bin, washes the mop pads in hot water, and dries them afterwards, which is the step that prevents the sour mildew smell that ruins mopping robots. The costs are real. It is expensive, the dock is a genuinely large appliance to house, and consumables run to a meaningful annual figure once you count pads, filters, brushes, and bags."
  },
  {
    "id": "dreame-x50-ultra",
    "name": "Dreame X50 Ultra",
    "category": "LiDAR, Retractable Mop, Step-Climbing",
    "badge": "Best Mopping",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2100,
    "pros": [
      "Genuinely climbs small thresholds and steps up to about 2.4 inches, which no other robot here manages",
      "Mop pads lift high enough to keep carpet dry rather than merely damp",
      "Strong downward pressure on the mop means it scrubs dried spills instead of smearing them",
      "Hot water washing and drying in the dock, with an effective self-cleaning routine",
      "Excellent hard-floor cleaning results on tile and sealed hardwood in our testing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Obstacle avoidance is good but a step behind the Roborock with small objects like cables",
      "The step-climbing mechanism adds height, so it does not fit under low furniture"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best mopping robot available, and the only one that solves split-level thresholds. Buy it for hard floors and door sills.",
    "description": "Two problems defeat most robot vacuums in real houses: thresholds between rooms, and mopping that merely spreads dirt around. The X50 Ultra is the first machine we have used that genuinely addresses both. Its climbing mechanism lifts the body over thresholds and small steps up to roughly 2.4 inches, which in a house with a raised kitchen or a sunken living room is the difference between one robot covering the floor plan and needing two. Mopping is where it separates itself further. The pads apply real downward pressure and rotate, so dried coffee and food spots come up rather than smearing, and in side-by-side runs on tile it left a visibly cleaner floor than the passive-drag mops that most robots use. The lifting mechanism raises the pads high enough that carpet stays dry when it transitions, which sounds like a small detail until you have owned a robot that leaves damp trails across a rug. Dock functions are complete: hot water washing, drying, and self-cleaning. Its weaknesses are the corollaries of its strengths. The climbing mechanism adds height, so it will not fit under the low furniture the Roborock slides beneath. And while obstacle avoidance is good, it is a clear step behind the Saros with small items like charging cables, which means slightly more floor tidying before a run."
  },
  {
    "id": "roomba-combo-10-max",
    "name": "iRobot Roomba Combo 10 Max",
    "category": "vSLAM + Dual Rubber Rollers, AutoWash Dock",
    "badge": "Best for Pet Hair",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 5600,
    "pros": [
      "Dual rubber roller design remains the best in the category for pet hair and carpet agitation",
      "Mop pad retracts fully into the body on carpet rather than lifting slightly, so nothing drags",
      "Dirt Detect genuinely works, making repeat passes where the sensor finds concentrated debris",
      "Long-established service and parts support \u2014 accessories are available everywhere",
      "The app's cleaning schedules and room routines are the most approachable for non-technical users"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Camera-based navigation is less precise than LiDAR in dim rooms and slower to map a floor",
      "Consumables are more expensive than competitors' and the ecosystem pushes subscriptions"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for carpeted homes with shedding pets. The rubber rollers do work that bristle brushes and fine suction numbers do not.",
    "description": "iRobot's rubber roller design has been copied widely and still executed best by the company that introduced it, and in a house with a shedding dog or cat on carpet that matters more than any specification on the box. Two counter-rotating rubber rollers agitate carpet fibres and lift embedded hair without the wrapping that plagues bristle brushes, and in our carpeted test area it consistently pulled more visible debris per pass than robots with higher suction ratings. Dirt Detect is the other feature that earns its place. An acoustic sensor identifies concentrated debris and the robot makes repeat passes over that spot rather than continuing at a uniform pace \u2014 genuinely useful under a dining table or by a back door. The AutoWash dock handles emptying and mop washing, and the mop pad fully retracts into the body on carpet rather than merely lifting, which eliminates damp trails. Navigation is where it trails the field. Camera-based vSLAM is less precise than LiDAR, slower to build a map, and noticeably weaker in dim rooms, so scheduled overnight runs are less reliable than with the Roborock or Dreame. Consumables are also more expensive than competitors', and the app pushes subscription services harder than we would like. For a carpeted home with pets, the cleaning performance still justifies choosing it."
  },
  {
    "id": "eufy-x10-pro-omni",
    "name": "eufy X10 Pro Omni",
    "category": "LiDAR, Twin Spinning Mops, Omni Dock",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 7400,
    "pros": [
      "Full omni dock \u2014 self-empty, mop wash, and dry \u2014 at roughly half the price of the flagships",
      "Twin spinning mop pads with real pressure clean hard floors far better than drag-pad designs",
      "LiDAR navigation and mapping are accurate and quick, with no meaningful gap to premium robots",
      "8,000 Pa suction handles low-pile carpet and hard floors comfortably",
      "Anti-tangle brush design copes well with moderate hair loads"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Obstacle avoidance is adequate rather than excellent \u2014 pick up cables before a run",
      "Mop pads are washed but dried with ambient air rather than heat, so drying is slower"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best price-to-capability ratio in the category. You give up some obstacle avoidance polish and save a great deal of money.",
    "description": "Two or three years ago, a self-emptying, self-washing, self-drying dock was a flagship feature. The X10 Pro Omni brings the whole arrangement to roughly half the price of the premium machines, and in day-to-day use the gap is much narrower than the price difference implies. Navigation is LiDAR-based, maps build quickly and accurately, and room-level control works properly \u2014 none of the compromises that used to define the mid-range. Mopping is its strongest suit. Twin spinning pads with genuine downward pressure scrub rather than wipe, and on sealed hardwood and tile the results were closer to the Dreame than to the passive-mop robots in the same price bracket. Suction is ample for hard floors and low-pile carpet, and the anti-tangle brush handles moderate hair without weekly intervention. Where it saves money is in refinement. Obstacle avoidance is adequate rather than excellent \u2014 it will move a charging cable rather than avoiding it, so a thirty-second floor tidy before a run is worth the habit. And the dock washes the pads but dries them with ambient air rather than heat, which takes longer and, in a humid house, is less effective at keeping them fresh. Neither is a reason to spend twice as much for most households."
  },
  {
    "id": "roborock-q-revo",
    "name": "Roborock Q Revo",
    "category": "LiDAR, Dual Spinning Mops, Empty + Wash Dock",
    "badge": "Best Budget Dock",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 6100,
    "pros": [
      "Self-emptying and mop-washing dock at a price where most robots still come with a plain charging base",
      "Roborock's mapping software is the best in the category and it is the same app here",
      "Dual spinning mops with pad lifting for carpet transitions",
      "Simple to live with \u2014 reliable scheduling and few of the quirks cheaper robots have",
      "Good parts availability and a large accessory ecosystem"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Camera-free obstacle avoidance means it relies on bump sensing for small objects",
      "Suction is lower than the flagships, which shows on thicker carpet"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The sweet spot for a household that wants a dock that empties and washes without paying flagship money. Tidy the floor first.",
    "description": "A robot vacuum without a self-emptying dock needs attention every two or three runs, which is precisely the chore people bought the robot to avoid. The Q Revo puts a self-emptying, mop-washing dock at a price point where competitors are still shipping a plain charging base, and that single fact makes it the most sensible entry into genuinely hands-off floor care. It runs Roborock's mapping software, which remains the best in the category \u2014 maps build quickly, rooms are identified sensibly, no-go zones actually work, and scheduling is reliable rather than something you check on. Dual spinning mops with lifting for carpet transitions cover the hard-floor side competently, and in ordinary use the difference from the flagship mopping systems is one of degree rather than kind. The compromise is obstacle handling. Without forward cameras, it detects objects by bumping into them, which is fine for chair legs and unhelpful for charging cables and socks. In a tidy house this is a non-issue; in a house with a toddler it means a quick sweep of the floor before each run. Suction is also lower than the flagships, which shows on medium and high-pile carpet \u2014 on hard floors and low pile it is entirely adequate."
  },
  {
    "id": "switchbot-k10-plus-pro",
    "name": "SwitchBot K10+ Pro",
    "category": "Ultra-Compact LiDAR, Self-Empty Dock",
    "badge": "Best for Small Spaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 2800,
    "pros": [
      "Under 10 inches across \u2014 reaches between chair legs and into corners that full-size robots cannot",
      "The whole dock has a footprint small enough for an apartment hallway",
      "Surprisingly strong suction for its size, and genuinely quiet in operation",
      "Accurate LiDAR mapping despite the compact body",
      "Self-emptying dock included, which is rare at this size and price"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Small dust bin means more frequent dock emptying cycles on a debris-heavy floor",
      "Mopping is a basic drag pad rather than a pressurised or spinning system"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right robot for an apartment or a furniture-dense home. It gets into places every other robot here simply cannot reach.",
    "description": "Every robot vacuum comparison assumes an open floor plan, and a great many people live in apartments full of chair legs, table bases, and narrow gaps that a 13-inch robot treats as walls. The K10+ Pro is under ten inches across, and the practical consequence is that it cleans the floor rather than the middle of the room \u2014 it threads between dining chairs, gets into the corner behind a door, and reaches under furniture that stops larger machines at the edge. The dock is proportionally small, which matters as much in an apartment as the robot's size does, and it still self-empties. Suction is better than the dimensions suggest, and the machine is genuinely quiet, which in a small home where you cannot walk away from the noise is a real consideration. LiDAR mapping is accurate and the app handles rooms and no-go zones properly. Its limits follow from its size. A small dust bin means more frequent trips back to the dock on a debris-heavy floor, which slows a full-house clean. And the mopping system is a basic drag pad \u2014 fine for light dust on sealed floors, not a substitute for the pressurised systems on the larger machines. For a one or two-bedroom apartment, those trade-offs buy access to floor area no other robot here can reach."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Robot Vacuums",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-robot-vacuum/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best robot vacuum in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Roborock Saros 10R is our overall pick \u2014 the best obstacle avoidance available, a body low enough to get under furniture, and a dock that washes and heat-dries the mop pads. For hard floors and thresholds, the Dreame X50 Ultra mops better and climbs steps. For carpeted homes with shedding pets, the Roomba Combo 10 Max's dual rubber rollers still lead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do robot vacuums replace a regular vacuum?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. A robot replaces the daily maintenance pass you would otherwise skip, keeping floors consistently tidy. It does not replace the deep clean a good upright performs on carpet, and it cannot do stairs, upholstery, or edges as thoroughly. Most households that are happy with a robot still own and use a conventional vacuum every few weeks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is more suction better in a robot vacuum?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only up to a point. Beyond roughly 5,000 Pa the difference on ordinary floors is hard to detect, and brush design, brush pressure and airflow path matter far more \u2014 which is why robots with lower Pascal ratings often out-clean higher-rated ones on carpet. Treat very high Pa claims as marketing rather than a buying criterion."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are self-emptying docks worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, more than any other upgrade. Without one you empty a small bin every two or three runs, which is exactly the chore the robot was supposed to remove. If the budget forces a choice between a better robot with a plain base and a simpler robot with a full dock, the dock is the better purchase."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can robot vacuums handle pet hair?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The good ones handle it well, and brush design is what decides it. Dual rubber rollers and anti-tangle designs lift hair without wrapping, while traditional bristle brushes need cutting free regularly. In a house with long-haired pets, brush type matters considerably more than suction rating or price."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much do robot vacuum consumables cost per year?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Budget $100 to $200 a year for a premium model in a busy home: dock bags every four to eight weeks, filters every two to three months, mop pads every three to six months, and brushes annually. Batteries last three to five years. Third-party consumables for popular models cost considerably less than branded ones."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do robot vacuums send data to the manufacturer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally yes. Floor plans sync to the cloud so the app works remotely, and camera-equipped models may upload images for obstacle recognition training. Several manufacturers now offer local-only processing or an opt-out for image uploads \u2014 check the settings, disable anything that sends photographs off the device if you would rather it did not, and use two-factor authentication on the account."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "best robot vacuum",
      "robot vacuum reviews",
      "self-emptying robot vacuum",
      "robot mop",
      "pet hair vacuum",
      "Roborock"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Robot Vacuums</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran six robots through a month of real houses — cables, socks, thresholds, pet hair and low furniture — and counted the thing that actually matters: how often we had to go and rescue one.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#roborock-saros-10r" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            Which Fits My Floors?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="27.5k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-they-do" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What They Replace</a>
          <a href="#navigation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Navigation &amp; Docks</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Match to Your Floors</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Specs That Matter</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Consumable Costs</a>
          <a href="#privacy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cameras &amp; Privacy</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Robot vacuums are sold on suction figures that stopped mattering several years ago. What decides whether one earns its place is how often you have to go and free it from a cable, whether it fits under the sofa, and whether the dock removes the chore or replaces it. Here is how six machines did in real, untidy houses.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="what-they-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Robot Vacuum Actually Replaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robot vacuum does not replace your vacuum cleaner. It replaces the daily maintenance sweep — the pass you would do every day if you had the time and never actually do. That is genuinely valuable in a house with pets, children, or hard floors, and it is a completely different job from the deep clean a good upright performs on carpet every few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Understood that way, the buying decision gets much clearer. You are not looking for maximum suction; you are looking for a machine that runs every day without needing to be rescued, empties itself, and does not create a new chore in place of the old one. The robots that fail in real houses fail on autonomy, not on cleaning power.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What We Measured</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rescues needed by the best robot in a month</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2.4"</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Highest threshold climbed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60-75 dB</p>
