          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Obstacle avoidance.</strong> The single strongest predictor of whether you keep using the robot. A machine that eats a charging cable once will be run manually afterwards, which defeats the purpose. Premium LiDAR-plus-vision systems are meaningfully better here, and it is the one place the money is clearly well spent.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brush design.</strong> Rubber rollers beat bristles for pet hair and carpet agitation; dual-roller and anti-tangle designs reduce the weekly ritual of cutting hair off the brush with scissors. In a house with long hair this matters more than suction by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mop lift height.</strong> A robot that lifts its pads 5 millimetres still dampens medium-pile carpet; one that lifts 10 millimetres or retracts the pad entirely does not. If you have both hard floors and rugs, this decides whether mopping is usable at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Robot height.</strong> Under three inches gets beneath most sofas and bed frames. Over four inches — common on robots with elaborate turret or climbing hardware — does not, and the dust under the furniture stays there.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dock functions.</strong> Self-empty is the minimum for genuine autonomy. Hot water pad washing plus heated drying is what stops mop pads smelling after a fortnight. Ambient-air drying works, more slowly, and struggles in humid houses.
          </p>
