        <div id="privacy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cameras, Maps, and Privacy
          </h2>

          <p>
            A modern robot vacuum builds a detailed floor plan of your home, and the camera-equipped models photograph the interior of every room at floor level. That data typically leaves the house — maps are synced to the manufacturer's cloud so the app works when you are away, and obstacle recognition images are sometimes uploaded for model training.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not a reason to avoid the category, but it is worth handling deliberately. Check whether the manufacturer offers local-only processing or lets you opt out of image uploads, both of which several brands now provide. Disable any "improve object recognition" setting that sends photographs off the device unless you are comfortable with it, and be aware that some models allow a remote video feed, which is a feature worth knowing about before it is enabled in a bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Account security matters more than people assume, because an account compromise exposes a map of your home and potentially a camera inside it. Use a unique password and enable two-factor authentication where the app supports it. If a manufacturer's privacy policy is vague about where images go, treat that as a specification failure rather than legal boilerplate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Keeping It Running Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            The weekly job is the brush and the sensors. Even anti-tangle designs collect some hair around the bearings, and two minutes with the supplied cutting tool prevents the gradual decline in pickup that owners tend to blame on the battery. Wipe the drop sensors on the underside at the same time — dust on those sensors makes a robot refuse to cross dark flooring, which is the most common false fault in the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monthly, wash the filter if the manufacturer says it is washable, and let it dry completely before refitting; a damp filter smells and restricts airflow. Clean the charging contacts on both the robot and the dock, because intermittent charging is nearly always contact contamination rather than a battery fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mopping models, take the pads off and machine wash them every few weeks regardless of what the dock does. Dock washing keeps them serviceable between cycles; it does not remove the accumulated grime that eventually makes a pad smell. Empty and rinse the dirty water tank rather than leaving it to stand, which is the actual cause of most complaints about odour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run a map rebuild after any significant furniture change. Robots navigate from a stored floor plan, and a map that no longer matches the room produces odd routing, missed areas, and repeated trips to a doorway that has moved. A rebuild takes one cleaning cycle and fixes a surprising share of complaints about a robot that "used to work better". The same applies after moving the dock, which is the reference point the whole map is anchored to, so put it somewhere permanent before you build the first map rather than relocating it later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, leave the robot docked and powered when it is not in use. Lithium batteries fare better kept topped up than repeatedly run flat, and a robot stored uncharged for months is the most common way owners end up needing a battery well before the three-year mark.
          </p>
        </div>


