            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best robot vacuum in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Roborock Saros 10R is our overall pick — the best obstacle avoidance available, a body low enough to get under furniture, and a dock that washes and heat-dries the mop pads. For hard floors and thresholds, the Dreame X50 Ultra mops better and climbs steps. For carpeted homes with shedding pets, the Roomba Combo 10 Max's dual rubber rollers still lead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums replace a regular vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A robot replaces the daily maintenance pass you would otherwise skip, keeping floors consistently tidy. It does not replace the deep clean a good upright performs on carpet, and it cannot do stairs, upholstery, or edges as thoroughly. Most households that are happy with a robot still own and use a conventional vacuum every few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is more suction better in a robot vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only up to a point. Beyond roughly 5,000 Pa the difference on ordinary floors is hard to detect, and brush design, brush pressure and airflow path matter far more — which is why robots with lower Pascal ratings often out-clean higher-rated ones on carpet. Treat very high Pa claims as marketing rather than a buying criterion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are self-emptying docks worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, more than any other upgrade. Without one you empty a small bin every two or three runs, which is exactly the chore the robot was supposed to remove. If the budget forces a choice between a better robot with a plain base and a simpler robot with a full dock, the dock is the better purchase.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can robot vacuums handle pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The good ones handle it well, and brush design is what decides it. Dual rubber rollers and anti-tangle designs lift hair without wrapping, while traditional bristle brushes need cutting free regularly. In a house with long-haired pets, brush type matters considerably more than suction rating or price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much do robot vacuum consumables cost per year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget $100 to $200 a year for a premium model in a busy home: dock bags every four to eight weeks, filters every two to three months, mop pads every three to six months, and brushes annually. Batteries last three to five years. Third-party consumables for popular models cost considerably less than branded ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums send data to the manufacturer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes. Floor plans sync to the cloud so the app works remotely, and camera-equipped models may upload images for obstacle recognition training. Several manufacturers now offer local-only processing or an opt-out for image uploads — check the settings, disable anything that sends photographs off the device if you would rather it did not, and use two-factor authentication on the account.
              </p>
            </div>

