            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Self-Emptying Dock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small onboard bin needs emptying every two or three runs. That is a new chore replacing the old one, and it is the most common reason robots end up unused in a cupboard. The dock is the upgrade that makes the category work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Measuring Furniture Clearance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Under the bed and the sofa is where the dust actually is. A robot too tall to get there leaves the dirtiest floor in the house untouched while reporting a completed clean. Measure your lowest clearance before choosing, not after.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Drag Pad to Mop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A passive pad towed behind the robot spreads a thin film of dirty water. Pressurised or spinning pad systems scrub, and the difference on tile is visible. If mopping is a real requirement, buy for pad pressure and pad washing rather than assuming all mopping is the same.
